In this lesson we'll look at three small families of identities that all come from the symmetry of the unit circle, and they're going to save you a ton of memorising. First up, the even and odd identities for negative angles. Cosine is an even function, so cosine of negative x just gives you cosine of x. But sine is odd, so sine of negative x flips the sign, and tangent does the same. Think of it like a mirror through the y-axis for cosine, and through the origin for sine and tangent. Next, supplementary angles, meaning angles that add up to pi. Sine behaves nicely and stays the same, but cosine and tangent both pick up a negative sign. So sine of pi minus x equals sine of x, while cosine of pi minus x equals negative cosine of x. Finally, complementary angles, which add up to pi over two. Here sine and cosine swap roles, so sine of pi over two minus x gives you cosine of x, and cosine of pi over two minus x gives you sine of x. Tangent turns into cotangent, the reciprocal of tangent. Once you spot which family an angle belongs to, the answer pops right out. Now let's see it in action.